<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rajahnate of Butuan</h1><p class="st-meta">1001 CE – 1756 CE (approx.) · capital Butuan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A gold-working trading state in northeastern Mindanao, which sent embassies to Song China from 1001 and built the famous balangay boats.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Butuan grew at the mouth of the Agusan River, where excavations have found plank boats called balangay, some dated to the 4th century and later. In 1001 it sent its first mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court, and Chinese records name rulers who sought the same status as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/champa">Champa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Butuan's wealth came from gold, worked into fine jewellery and regalia found in large hoards, and from trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/champa">Champa</a> and the Malay world. In 1007 its ruler Kiling asked for equal rank with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and in 1011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-bata-shaja">Sri Bata Shaja</a> sent another embassy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-tara-of-agusan">Golden Tara of Agusan</a> suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> influence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Butuan's prominence faded by the 14th century as trade shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> and Sulu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-siagu">Rajah Siagu</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan's</a> expedition in 1521. Butuan came gradually under Spanish rule, and its archaeology is now central to Philippine national history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rajahnate-of-butuan.jpg" alt="Rajahnate of Butuan" width="320" height="446"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Butuan_1521.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1001 CE – 1756 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Butuan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kiling, Sri Bata Shaja, Rajah Siagu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion, Hindu-Buddhist influence</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Butuanon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Butuan, Kingdom of Butuan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1378&amp;focus=rajahnate-of-butuan" aria-label="Rajahnate of Butuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8147 1843 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rajahnate of Butuan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8467 2099L7901 1174M8467 2099L8309 1890"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8467 2099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8467 2099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Butuan. <a href="/atlas?year=1378&amp;focus=rajahnate-of-butuan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

